10748218,2024-11-10,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"Request to Remove Student Loans from Credit Report Dear Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, I am writing to formally request the removal of certain student loan accounts from my credit report under provisions of the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act ( FERPA ) ( 20 USC 1232g ) and the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) ( 15 USC 1681 ). 

1. FERPA and Privacy of Student Records : FERPA protects the privacy of student education records and restricts unauthorized disclosures. Upon reviewing my credit report, it appears that my student loan information has been disclosed to credit reporting agencies in a manner that may violate FERPA. To comply with FERPA, I request : - An investigation because student loan information was disclosed without appropriate consent. 
- Immediate removal of any disclosed student loan information from my credit report. 

2. FCRA and Consumer Privacy : Under 15 USC 1681 section 602 ( a ), consumer reporting agencies are obligated to handle consumer information with fairness, impartiality, and respect for privacy. Furthermore : - 15 USC 6801 states financial institutions must respect the privacy and confidentiality of customer information. 
- 15 USC 1681 section 604 ( a ) ( 2 ) specifies that consumer reporting agencies may only furnish a consumer report with written consent from the consumer. I have not, and do not, provide such consent. 

