10883861,2024-11-22,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Problem with a company's investigation into an existing problem,Their investigation did not fix an error on your report,"On XXXX XX/XX/XXXX Complainant filed a direct dispute ( CFPB XXXX ) against regarding ECSI 's furnishing of false, misleading and inaccurate information of XXXX ( XXXX ) private student loan for inclusion in the files XXXX, XXXX, and XXXX maintain and disseminate regarding Complainant. Specifically, Complainant identified several pieces of information furnished by ECSI to the CRAs that were facially inaccurate, incomplete and/or misleading. On XXXX XX/XX/XXXX ECSI responded to the direct dispute with generic language stating in part, "" As a XXXX  party servicer, ECSI, is required to maintain the integrity of the credit information provided to the XXXX major credit bureaus. The Fair Credit Reporting Act requires furnishers of information to provide accurate information to the reporting agencies. '' On XXXX XX/XX/XXXX Complainant filed another direct dispute ( CFPB XXXX ) and attached a copy of a XXXX XX/XX/XXXX billing statement that simultaneously said the account was XXXX days past-due and reported to the CRAs with a rating of 30 days past-due. This made the status reported to the CRAs facially inaccurate and misleading. Despite receiving this evidence, ECSI refused to correct the facial inaccuracies their systems reported regarding Complainant to the CRAs. In fact, ECSI responded on or around XXXX XX/XX/XXXX, stating "" Although ECSI conducted an investigation of [ Complainant 's ] account in response to his initial request, as a courtesy we have completed an additional review of the account and found no discrepancies between the account status and the credit reporting. Our records indicate the activity on your loan was reported accurately to the credit bureaus and no further action is required on the part of ECSI. As [ Complainant ] has not provided any additional information or supporting documentation to justify his assertions, we are unable to make changes to the credit report. "" However, that is NOT the standard. The standard requires that information furnished to the credit reporting agencies under 1681s-2 be both accurate and *not*materially misleading ( emphasis added ). ECSI continued in its response to CFPB XXXX, stating "" Our records reflect the activity on your loan was reported accurately to the credit bureaus. Any adjustments made to your credit report that are not warranted would compromise the integrity of the data. We have verified that no error in reporting has occurred and we will continue to report the factual credit history on your loan. '' However, the integrity of the data was already compromised by ECSI itself. In Complainant 's notice of dispute, ( CFPB XXXX ) Complainant identified specific issues with the information ECSI furnished to the CRAs for the months of XX/XX/XXXX and XX/XX/XXXX. Had ECSI conducted a cursory examination of the billing statements it would have found several billing irregularity, which appear to correlate with the inaccurate information reported for the XXXX ( XXXX ) accounts ECSI furnishes to the CRAs regarding Complainant. For example, a billing statement generated on XXXX XX/XX/XXXX has a statement due-date of XXXX XX/XX/XXXX. This does not fall in-line with standard practices for statement generation for private student loans which is typically at least XXXX ( XXXX ) days between statement generation date and statement due date. For exposition, TILA/Regulation Z requires a policy for credit card accounts of not less than twenty-one ( XXXX ) days between statement generation date and statement due-date. Now, the same statement with the abridged interval between generation date and due date also alleges that at time of statement generation ( XXXX XX/XX/XXXX ) the account was fifteen ( XXXX ) days past-due. Yet, only XXXX ( XXXX ) days had passed between the previous statement due-date ( XXXX XX/XX/XXXX ) and the new statement generation date ( XXXX XX/XX/XXXX ), calling into question the integrity of ECSI 's billing cycle policies and data verification systems. Finally, and perhaps egregious, even under the supposition that somehow XXXX ( XXXX ) days had elapsed between XXXX XX/XX/XXXX and XXXX XX/XX/XXXX, XXXX provided the following information regarding what it was furnishing to the CRAs regarding the account status at that time : "" Credit Bureau rating as of the above due date : Past Due 30 Days ''. Hence, the XXXX issues, payment history issues, and payment status issues that Complainant identified were a directly related to the billing error issues for the identified statement. Clearly, ECSI failed as a data furnisher under 15 U.S.C. 1681s-2 ( b ) ( 1 ) ( A ) which mandates furnishers conduct an investigation of disputed information. In addition, ECSIs practices contravene the requirements outlined in Appendix E to Part XXXX of Title XXXX, which sets forth the Interagency Guidelines Concerning the Accuracy and Integrity of Information Furnished to Consumer Reporting Agencies. The guidelines clearly mandate that furnishers, such as ECSI, must establish a system for furnishing information about consumers to consumer reporting agencies that is appropriate to the nature, size, complexity, and scope of the furnishers business operations. Specifically, ECSI is obligated to : Establish and implement appropriate internal controls regarding the accuracy and integrity of information about consumers furnished to consumer reporting agencies, such as by implementing standard procedures and verifying random samples of information provided to consumer reporting agencies ( Appendix E ( d ) ). Provide for appropriate and effective oversight of relevant service providers whose activities may affect the accuracy or integrity of information about consumers furnished to consumer reporting agencies to ensure compliance with the policies and 