ncompliance with the FCRA. This willful disregard for consumer protection law and regulation exposes ECSI to liability for statutory damages, as well as actual and punitive damages, under 15 U.S.C. 1681n and 15 U.S.C. 1681o. The FCRA permits recovery for humiliation and mental distress, as well as for reputational and creditworthiness injuries, as outlined in XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX ( XXXX XXXX. XXXX ). Moreover, XXXX v. XXXX XXXX. XXXX, XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX. XXXX XXXX ( XXXX. Mich. XXXX ) recognized that emotional distress is a sufficiently concrete injury to support XXXX XXXX standing in an FCRA claim. Likewise, in XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX. XXXX XXXX ( XXXX. XXXX XXXX ), the court affirmed that emotional distress constitutes an injury in fact under the FCRA, even in light of the XXXX decision. Additionally, the XXXX XXXX 's ruling in XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, No. XXXX ( XXXX Cir. XXXX ), confirmed that statutory damages are recoverable under 15 U.S.C. 1681n ( a ) ( 1 ) ( A ), even in the absence of actual harm, when violations are willful.ECSI was provided with the XXXX, and XXXX precedents, a copy of Appendix E to part XXXX, CFPB Advisory Opinion on Facially false data, and a copy of the XXXX XXXX. ECSI also received the supporting documentation of for the XXXX  XXXX after the documentation was provided to XXXX on or around XXXX XX/XX/XXXX. ECSI knew or had reasonable cause to believe the information they were furnishing for the respective accounts was inaccurate because by XXXX XX/XX/XXXX ECSI changed the reporting for the information they supplied to XXXX regarding Complainant. Whereas a XXXX XX/XX/XXXX XXXX credit report indicated a purported delinquency of XXXX  days for XX/XX/XXXX ( see XXXX Report with Confirmation XXXX ), the reporting by XXXX XX/XX/XXXX indicates a purported delinquency changed from XX/XX/XXXX to XX/XX/XXXX ( see XXXX report with Confirmation # XXXX ). Thus, ECSI is not only violating t