er protection standards. XXXXXXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX Violation : These student loans are being reported without clarity on terms or proper disclosures. Additionally, 18 U.S.C. 894 applies here, as XXXX is attempting to collect without providing lawful validation or proof of the debts accuracy. Hard Inquiries XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXXXX/XX/XXXX ) Violation : These inquiries were performed without my explicit written consent, violating 15 U.S.C. 1681b, which governs permissible purposes for accessing a consumer report. Key Violations and Legal Arguments 1. Misrepresentation of Accounts ( Auto Loan as Credit Sale ) : XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX misrepresented a credit sale as an auto loan. Misclassifications harm consumers by inflating debt-to-income ratios and misrepresenting their financial standing. Such practices are actionable under : 18 U.S.C. 894 : Collection by extortionate means, including coercive misrepresentation. FTC Act ( 15 U.S.C. 45 ( a ) ) : Prohibits unfair or deceptive practices in commerce. 2. Failure to Provide Disclosures and Informed Consent : Both XXXX  and the XXXX  XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX provide the following essential disclosures, rendering their agreements ambiguous and invalid : What will be reported ( e.g., payment history, balances ). When it will be reported ( e.g., monthly, upon delinquency ). How reporting affects creditworthiness ( e.g., score impact ). Without these disclosures, consumers can not provide informed consent. This is a violation of : 15 U.S.C. 1681b : Requires permissible purpose for reporting. XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX ) : Establishes the need for explicit consumer consent for reporting. 3. Failure to Block Unauthorized Information ( FCRA 605B ) : The accounts in question were disputed through an FTC Identity Theft Report. Under 15 U.S.C. 1681c-2, credit reporting agencies are required to block disputed information with